Instructions
============

1. in terminal, "pip install selenium"

2. in code
    a. "from selenium import webdriver" - if the yellow lined appear don't worry it just says the installation file is not found yet but it will be fixed when the code runs
    b. set up the chrome web driver service - "from selenium.webdriver.chrome.service import Service"

3. This is a tool which allows us to a particular browser as a real user does

4. Download the chrome driver (it should match with your chrome version) - https://googlechromelabs.github.io/chrome-for-testing/#stable - watch for chrome driver (not chrome)

5. Place the chrome driver .exe in the same folder as the python file

6. After configuring the executable path, we can grab any website

==============================================
test_1 - Search something
==========================
* Search the element within the HTML
* When having the access to the element interact with it (click, change etc.)
    * Find the google search field
    * Click it
    * Type some thing '....'
    * Hit enter
===============================================
Challenges Faced
=================
* The testing sites might ban your Ip or block your
* You might be able to able to solve some captcha to verify whether you are a bot or not (in this case you are a bot, so you won't be able to confirm)
* Redirect you
===============================================
How to do this in multiple instances?
=====================================
* Push the code in to the cloud and to for multiple instance all at once


===============================================
Better tools for future
=======================
*Bright Data - A tool which can run multiple instances, solves captcha automatically and rotate IPs to avoid getting banned
=================================================
Terminology
===========
* boilerplate - A boilerplate is a standard, reusable piece of text, code, or formatting that can be copied and used over and over with little or no changes
================================================
* What is XPATH, is it better than CLASSNAME ?
    It's a special syntax which can be used to search a specific test/attribute
